# ws-reduce — exact server-side aggregate reduce

Base `merge/transport`. Correctness first: exact integer SUM (the server's
`sum(bigint)` is numeric), AVG, text/date/time/numeric MIN/MAX, COUNT on every
kind, and an FPREDUCE v2 frame that carries int128 and text/numeric values.
The existing sum/count/min/max pushdown and the client-side fallback both
remain, and both now produce the same values.

## Command (same command, baseline then change, one at a time under the lock)

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker build -q --target dev -t fpq-reduce -f docker/Dockerfile .'
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline \
      -v "$PWD/bench/jobs/aggregate-pushdown.py:/src/bench/jobs/aggregate-pushdown.py:ro" \
      fpq-baseline python3 /src/bench/jobs/aggregate-pushdown.py'
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=reduce \
      -v "$PWD/bench/jobs/aggregate-pushdown.py:/src/bench/jobs/aggregate-pushdown.py:ro" \
      fpq-reduce python3 /src/bench/jobs/aggregate-pushdown.py'

2,000,000 rows (`fp_agg_bench`), `SELECT price FROM fp_agg_bench` reduced with
`sum(price)` + `count_all`, verified against `SELECT sum(price), count(*)` on
the server. Two shapes: the driver's streaming batch (100k) and one request for
the whole result (2M). `bytes` is what the worker actually published in the data
segment, measured by wrapping the frame reader.

