### AB

The baseline image predates the reduce op, so its `aggregate()` walks the
columnar batches in C++ after the worker ships them; the change accumulates in
the worker and publishes scalars. Same command, same query, same `ok` verdict
(price_sum=999000000, rows=2000000 on both).

| shape (2M rows) | baseline | ws/reduce | delta |
|---|---|---|---|
| batch=100k | 206.45 ms, 9.69M rows/s, 16,251,040 B | 183.53 ms, 10.90M rows/s, 2,016 B | +12.5% rows/s, -99.99% bytes |
| batch=2M (one request) | 167.78 ms, 11.92M rows/s, 16,250,056 B | 155.05 ms, 12.90M rows/s, 192 B | +8.2% rows/s, -99.999% bytes |

Host noise on these cells is ±1-2% (the baseline batch=100k cell moved 194.71
-> 206.45 ms between runs of the same image). The byte counts are exact: the
worker's frame is 96 B per request (two ops), where the baseline shipped the
whole int8 column (16 MB). The throughput delta is that traffic plus the
decode/transpose the baseline's C++ reduce still has to walk.

Correctness is covered by the suite, not inferred from this number:
`python/tests/test_aggregate.py` checks every shape against the server
(exact bigint sum past int64, avg on int/float/numeric and the server's numeric
division scale, text min/max under the server collation, date/time min/max,
numeric min/max/sum, count on text, empty results, and pushdown == fallback).
